in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some IOT devices find  their long-lost friends is friendship  the best configuration for performance  stay tuned to find out one of the big  challenges with IOT devices is that  there's a tendency for each device to  use a different set of protocols for  communicating with other devices around  them this is where I ot exchange comes  in their product is somewhat like a  dating app for IOT devices where a  device can find other local devices that  communicate on similar protocols their  system was running on a federation of  vm's inside of google cloud using  persistent disks to host relational  databases that they were using to keep  everything in sync sadly after some  profiling they realized they weren't  getting the performance they wanted out  of the disk itself now if you remember  persistent disks are the ones that  aren't physically attached to your host  vm hardware and they come in two flavors  standard and SSD that each one has their  own performance characteristics which  unsurprisingly matters a lot for your  usage configuration now for persistent  disks in general performance scales as a  function of the size of the disk the  bigger it is the faster it is we can see  here that for a standard persistent disk  the IEEE opsin throughput scale pretty  well in relation to the size of the disk  SSD persistent disks show the same  behavior although the numbers are  significantly higher however SSD  persistent disks also have performance  scale with the number of the CPUs that  the VM has or as the the docks a SSD  persistent disk performance scales  linearly until it reaches either the  limits of the volume or the limits of  each compute engine instance which was  where the IOT exchange folks were  running into trouble their VMs didn't  have enough CPUs for the IEEE ops they  needed to their SSD pd's by moving them  from eight core to 32 core machines they  saw their I up performance almost double  you gotta love it when data saves the  day if you'd like to know more about  today's topic check out the article  listed in the description below and  don't forget to check out the rest of  our cloud performance Atlas content on  YouTube because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

